{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent figure in the Crusades and a ruler of the County of Tripoli from 1155 to 1180. He was a member of the Raymond dynasty who had held the position for several generations. Raymond III inherited the county at a young age and faced significant challenges including internal rebellions and external threats from Muslim powers. His reign was marked by military campaigns and diplomatic efforts to maintain the county's independence.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III's early life was largely shaped by the political instability of the period. He was the son of Raymond IV Count of Tripoli and his wife Adele of Montferrat. He ascended to the county after his father's death in 1155 a period characterized by a power struggle within the family. Raymond III initially relied on the support of his uncle William of Montferrat to stabilize the county. He demonstrated shrewd political skills navigating the complex relationships between the various Crusader states and local factions.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw a focus on strengthening the county's defenses and expanding its territory. He engaged in numerous military campaigns against Muslim forces controlling parts of Syria and Palestine. These campaigns were often costly and involved significant losses but they helped to solidify Tripoli's position as a major Crusader power. He also invested in infrastructure and economic development attempting to improve the lives of his subjects.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* A defining aspect of Raymond III’s rule was his involvement in numerous military conflicts. He led expeditions against the Ayyubid dynasty particularly Saladin’s forces. These conflicts were frequently protracted and involved significant logistical challenges. Raymond III’s military successes were often attributed to his skilled leadership and the loyalty of his troops. He also participated in campaigns against other Muslim territories and defended Tripoli against sieges. His military endeavors expanded the county's territory and secured its borders.\n\nRaymond III's reign wasn't without its internal challenges. He faced opposition from rebellious nobles and factions who sought to undermine his authority. He had to contend with economic difficulties and maintain stability within his county. Despite these challenges Raymond III proved to be a capable and effective ruler.\n\n*Legacy and Later Life* Raymond III died in 1180 leaving behind a stable county and a legacy of military strength. He is remembered as a key figure in the Crusades who played a significant role in defending the Crusader states. His reign saw the County of Tripoli reach its peak in terms of territorial extent and military power. After his death his son Raymond IV succeeded him ensuring the Raymond dynasty's continued rule. Raymond III's contributions to the Crusader states and his efforts to maintain Tripoli's independence cemented his place in Crusader history. He is often portrayed as a strong and courageous leader who defended the Christian cause during a turbulent period.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1434580385684967}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -5.4834770708112046e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.09451551735401154}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.03654785081744194}, {"token": "▁prominent", "logprob": -1.340708613395691}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -0.020277930423617363}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.005189164076000452}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.03926732763648033}, {"token": "▁Crus", "logprob": -1.0677392482757568}, {"token": "ades", "logprob": -0.4741100072860718}, {"token": "▁and", "logprob": -0.3818128705024719}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.10064152628